-- title:
--   The projection $\pi_X$, history $\sigma$-algebras, the weighted average $\bar x^s$ and subgradients $\partial F(x)$
-- statement:
--   This file fixes the objects in which the stochastic quasigradient (SQG) projection method of Pflug (Ch. 17) and Uryasev (Ch. 18) is stated. Throughout, $\mathbb R^n$ carries the Euclidean norm $\|\cdot\|$ and inner product $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$.
--
--   1. **Projection.** For $X\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ and $y\in\mathbb R^n$, $\pi_X(y)$ is a point of $X$ nearest to $y$:
--   $$
--   \pi_X(y)\in\operatorname{argmin}\{\|y-x\| : x\in X\}.
--   $$
--   For $X$ nonempty, closed and convex it exists and is unique.
--   2. **Noise history.** For random elements $\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots$, $\sigma(\xi_0,\dots,\xi_{t-1})$ is the smallest $\sigma$-algebra making them measurable (the trivial one for $t=0$).
--   3. **Process history.** For random vectors $x^0,x^1,\dots$ and $\xi^0,\xi^1,\dots$, the $\sigma$-algebra induced by $(x^0,\dots,x^s,\xi^0,\dots,\xi^{t-1})$. With $t=s$ it is the $\sigma$-algebra $B_s$ of Theorem 1 of Chapter 18; with $t=s+1$ it also contains $\xi^s$.
--   4. **Weighted average** (Eq. (18.6)). For stepsizes $\rho_0,\rho_1,\dots$ and points $x^0,x^1,\dots$,
--   $$
--   \bar x^s=\sum_{\ell=0}^s\rho_\ell x^\ell\Big/\sum_{\ell=0}^s\rho_\ell .
--   $$
--   5. **Subgradients.** For $F:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$, a set $D$ and $x$, $\partial_D F(x)=\{h : F(x)+\langle h,y-x\rangle\le F(y)\ \text{for all }y\in D\}$. For a convex $F$ on an open convex $D\ni x$ this is the subdifferential $\partial F(x)$ of Chapter 18.
--
--   These are the objects in which the recursion $x^{s+1}=\pi_X(x^s-\rho_s\xi^s)$ (18.2), the adaptive stepsize rule (18.5), and Theorems 1 and 2 of Chapter 18 and the Theorem of Chapter 17 are stated.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathbb R^n$ is `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)`. `projX X y` is a chosen nearest point when one exists and $y$ otherwise; every statement of the mission assumes $X$ nonempty and closed. The weighted average divides by $\sum_{\ell\le s}\rho_\ell$ and is $0$ when that sum vanishes; every statement that uses it has positive stepsizes (almost surely). The histories are suprema of pull-backs of the Borel $\sigma$-algebra.
-- source:
--   S. Uryasev, "Adaptive Stochastic Quasigradient Procedures", in Ermoliev & Wets (eds.), Numerical Techniques for Stochastic Optimization, Springer 1988, Ch. 18, p. 373, Eq. (18.2) (π_X); p. 375, Eq. (18.6) (weighted average); p. 376, Theorem 1 (σ-algebras of conditions (1), (2)); p. 377, Theorem 2 (∂F(x)); G. Ch. Pflug, "Stepsize Rules, Stopping Times and their Implementation in Stochastic Quasigradient Algorithms", ibid., Ch. 17, p. 355, Eq. (17.4) (Π_S), p. 356 (𝓕_n)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_NumStochOpt_QuasiFejer_ProjectionMethod

open MeasureTheory
open scoped InnerProductSpace

namespace NumStochOpt.Stepsize

/-- The σ-algebra `σ(ξ₀, …, ξ_{t-1})` generated by the first `t` terms of a random sequence
(`⊥` for `t = 0`). -/
def noiseSigma {Ω Ξ : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ξ] (ξ : ℕ → Ω → Ξ) (t : ℕ) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ (k : ℕ) (_ : k < t), MeasurableSpace.comap (ξ k) inferInstance

/-- The σ-algebra induced by `(x⁰, …, x^s, ξ⁰, …, ξ^{t-1})` (Ch. 18, p. 376). With `t = s` it is
the history `B_s` of Theorem 1, condition (1); with `t = s + 1` it is the σ-algebra of condition
(2). -/
def histSigma {Ω : Type*} {n : ℕ} (x ξ : ℕ → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (s t : ℕ) :
    MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  (⨆ (k : ℕ) (_ : k ≤ s), MeasurableSpace.comap (x k) inferInstance) ⊔ noiseSigma ξ t

/-- The weighted (Cesàro) average `x̄^s = ∑_{ℓ=0}^s ρ_ℓ x^ℓ / ∑_{ℓ=0}^s ρ_ℓ` of Eq. (18.6)
(Ch. 18, p. 375). When `∑_{ℓ ≤ s} ρ_ℓ = 0` the value is the junk value `0`. -/
noncomputable def weightedAvg {n : ℕ} (ρ : ℕ → ℝ) (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (s : ℕ) :
    EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) :=
  (∑ ℓ ∈ Finset.range (s + 1), ρ ℓ)⁻¹ • ∑ ℓ ∈ Finset.range (s + 1), ρ ℓ • x ℓ

/-- The set of subgradients of `F` at `x` relative to a domain `D`:
`∂F(x) = {h : F(x) + ⟨h, y - x⟩ ≤ F(y) for all y ∈ D}`. For a convex `F` defined on an open
convex set `D ∋ x` this is the usual subdifferential (Ch. 18, p. 377). -/
def subdiffOn {n : ℕ} (D : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))) (F : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ)
    (x : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) :=
  {h | ∀ y ∈ D, F x + ⟪h, y - x⟫_ℝ ≤ F y}

end NumStochOpt.Stepsize


